+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — unicode-normalization 0.1.25
+
+Upstream: unicode-rs/unicode-normalization @ 576ae0b1407dd14854876c93f1a348df0c19dffe
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All tests live in `src/test.rs` (module `test::hegel_props`, gated on the
+`std` feature because hegeltest needs std; the crate itself is
+no_std-capable). They share two composite generators:
+
+- `interesting_char()` — full-Unicode codepoints boosted toward
+  normalization-sensitive regions: combining diacritics, precomposed
+  Latin, Hangul syllables (U+AC00..U+D7A3) *and* bare conjoining jamo
+  (U+1100..U+11FF, so LV+T and L+V composition across drawn characters
+  happens), CJK compatibility ideographs (singleton decompositions),
+  halfwidth/fullwidth forms, plus a sampled list of specifics: NFC_QC=No
+  characters (U+0340/0341/0343/0344), singletons (U+2126, U+212B), CGJ,
+  the huge compat expansion U+FDFA, Tibetan multi-part vowels
+  (U+0F73/0F75, which decompose to two nonstarters), etc.
+- `unicode_string()` — up to 8 segments of interesting chars, each
+  optionally followed by a run of `combining_mark()`s of either 1–6 marks
+  (canonical-ordering-sensitive) or 28–36 marks (straddling the
+  stream-safe MAX_NONSTARTERS = 30 boundary). `combining_mark()` mixes
+  U+0300..U+036F with marks of unusual ccc (1, 7, 8, 33, 103, 129, 130,
+  132, 216, 226, 228, 240) and CGJ (ccc 0, blocks reordering).
+
+The `fuzz/` directory already covers no-panic and one direction of
+quick-check implication; these properties target the semantic UAX #15 laws
+it doesn't.
+
+Seventeen tests, ten distinct property families:
+
+1. **Idempotence** (`prop_nfc_idempotent`, `prop_nfd_idempotent`,
+   `prop_nfkc_idempotent`, `prop_nfkd_idempotent`) — F(F(x)) == F(x) for
+   each of the four forms. Evidence: definitional in UAX #15.
+2. **Round-trip stability** (`prop_canonical_roundtrip_stability`) —
+   NFC(NFD(x)) == NFC(x) and NFD(NFC(x)) == NFD(x): x, NFD(x), NFC(x) are
+   canonically equivalent so must share both canonical forms. Evidence:
+   the UAX #15 conformance clauses mirrored by `tests/tests.rs::test_official`
+   on the fixed NormalizationTest.txt vectors — these generalize them to
+   generated inputs.
+3. **Inclusion / compatibility subsumes canonical**
+   (`prop_nfkc_subsumes_canonical`, `prop_nfkd_subsumes_canonical`) —
+   NFKC(x) == NFKC(NFC(x)) == NFKC(NFD(x)) == NFKC(NFKD(x)), and the NFKD
+   analogues. Same conformance-clause evidence.
+4. **Quick-check consistency** (`prop_quick_check_{nfc,nfd,nfkc,nfkd}_consistent`)
+   — `is_nfX_quick` returning Yes implies F(x) == x, No implies
+   F(x) != x, Maybe implies nothing; and the authoritative `is_nfX(x)`
+   equals `F(x) == x`. Evidence: the enum's documented meaning in
+   `src/quick_check.rs`. The quick-check tables and the full
+   decompose/recompose pipeline are independent oracles for each other.
+5. **Stream vs whole** (`prop_iterator_adapters_match_str_methods`) — the
+   blanket `impl UnicodeNormalization for I: Iterator<Item = char>`
+   (reached via `.chars().map(|c| c)`) and the `impl for char` agree with
+   the `&str` methods. Evidence: the existing `test_nfd` macro checks the
+   dummy-iterator path on fixed examples.
+6. **Concatenation at a stable code point**
+   (`prop_concat_at_stable_code_point`) — normalization is *not* closed
+   under arbitrary concatenation, so this tests exactly the documented
+   stable-boundary version (UAX #15 §9.1): if the second piece begins with
+   a stable code point (ccc == 0 and per-char quick-check == Yes for the
+   drawn form), then F(a + b) == F(a) + F(b). Stability of the drawn
+   boundary char is checked via the public API (`canonical_combining_class`
+   + `is_nfX_quick` on a one-char iterator), falling back to ASCII `'x'`
+   when the candidate isn't stable.
+7. **Hangul algorithmic roundtrip** (`prop_hangul_algorithmic_roundtrip`)
+   — the UAX #15 §3.12 Hangul arithmetic, transcribed into the test as a
+   spec oracle: for drawn L/V(/T) jamo, NFC of the jamo sequence equals
+   the arithmetically computed syllable, NFD of the syllable equals the
+   jamo sequence, `decompose_canonical` agrees, and `compose` composes
+   stepwise (L+V → LV, LV+T → LVT).
+8. **`compose` produces a canonical equivalent**
+   (`prop_compose_result_canonically_equivalent`) — if
+   `compose(a, b) == Some(c)` then NFD(c) == NFD(a·b) and
+   NFC(c) == NFC(a·b). Evidence: `compose` is documented as canonical
+   character composition; a primary composite is canonically equivalent to
+   its decomposition. When `compose` returns None the case passes
+   trivially, so the pair generator has a dedicated composable branch
+   (ASCII letters × U+0300..U+030F accents, Hangul L × V) — see Test
+   results for why.
+9. **Structural invariants of decomposed output**
+   (`prop_nfd_output_fully_decomposed_and_ordered`,
+   `prop_nfkd_output_fully_decomposed_and_ordered`) — every char in
+   NFD/NFKD output decomposes to itself under
+   `decompose_canonical`/`decompose_compatible` (fully decomposed), and
+   ccc never strictly decreases within a nonstarter run (Canonical
+   Ordering Algorithm). Checked with the char-level lookups directly,
+   independent of the iterator's buffer-and-sort implementation in
+   `src/decompose.rs`.
+10. **Stream-safe process** (`prop_stream_safe_idempotent`,
+    `prop_stream_safe_only_inserts_cgj`) — UAX15-D4 is idempotent, and its
+    only edit is inserting CGJ: for CGJ-free input, deleting CGJs from the
+    output reproduces the input exactly. The 28–36-mark runs in the
+    generator make the insertion path fire routinely.
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full suite green: 32 lib tests (15 pre-existing + 17 hegel) plus all
+  integration tests (`tests/tests.rs` official conformance suite,
+  `cjk_compat_variants`, `public_assigned`, streamsafe regression) pass.
+- Liveness verified twice, with one real catch:
+  - Inverting the NFC idempotence assertion produced a failure with a
+    fully shrunk counterexample.
+  - Inverting the compose-equivalence assertion initially *still passed*:
+    with `a` drawn from `interesting_char()` and `b` from marks, the
+    `compose(a, b) == Some(_)` branch was never reached in a default
+    100-case run — the test was vacuous. Fixed by adding a targeted
+    composable branch to the pair generator (ASCII letter + combining
+    accent, Hangul L + V); the inverted assertion then failed and shrank
+    to the minimal composable pair `('A', '\u{300}')`. Reverted after.
+  - `.hegel/` DB deleted after the checks and added to `.gitignore`.
+- Exploratory 10x run: all 17 properties at `test_cases = 1000` — green
+  (2.5 s). Committed tests are back at the default 100.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Every property held at 10x case counts. This is consistent
+with the crate's maturity: it ships the full official
+NormalizationTest.txt conformance suite as a unit test and has three
+libfuzzer targets (no-panic, quick-check implication, NFC lookahead
+discipline) that have presumably had substantial fuzz time. The new tests
+still add value the fuzzers don't get in CI: they run under plain
+`cargo test`, and they check laws the fuzz targets omit (idempotence,
+canonical-equivalence stability, subsumption, stable-point concatenation,
+the Hangul arithmetic oracle, canonical ordering of output).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Concatenation closure for arbitrary splits** was never asserted —
+  UAX #15 is explicit that no normalization form is closed under
+  concatenation (e.g. NFC("e") + NFC("\u{301}") != NFC("e\u{301}")). Only
+  the documented stable-code-point version is tested (property 6).
+- **"Stable" defined as ccc == 0 alone** would be unsound for NFC: Hangul
+  V/T jamo and composing marks with ccc 0... more precisely, quick-check
+  Maybe characters (e.g. V jamo U+1161) are starters yet compose with a
+  preceding LV/L, so the per-form quick-check == Yes conjunct in
+  property 6 is load-bearing; dropping it makes the test fail on
+  `a = "\u{1100}", boundary = "\u{1161}"`-shaped inputs.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's Setup section anticipated this crate exactly:
+  `rust-version = "1.36"` made plain `cargo add --dev hegeltest` fail, and
+  the documented `--ignore-rust-version` retry worked first time.
+- The codepoint-drawing idiom from the reference (integers → `char::from_u32`
+  → `unwrap_or` fallback, weighted with `one_of!`) plus the SKILL.md
+  boundary-conjunction guidance (explicit 28–36 mark runs to straddle
+  MAX_NONSTARTERS = 30) composed cleanly; uniform `text()` would
+  essentially never produce >30-nonstarter runs or L·V jamo adjacency.
+- Everything compiled on the first attempt; no API mismatches between the
+  reference and hegeltest 0.28.2.
+- Minor friction: `sampled_from(vec![...])` over integer literals needs a
+  type suffix on the first element (`0x0334_u32`) — unsurprising Rust
+  inference, but worth a one-line mention near the `sampled_from` docs
+  since codepoint tables are a common use.
+- The liveness check earned its keep: it exposed a *vacuous conditional
+  property* (a `if let Some(c) = compose(a, b)` branch never taken at the
+  default case count). SKILL.md's "verify the tests are live" step only
+  says to invert an assertion; it might be worth a sentence saying that
+  for properties guarded by a condition, the inversion must be inside the
+  guarded branch — that's what distinguishes "the test can fail" from
+  "the interesting branch is ever reached".
